New evidence

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Chapter 3: The Federal Government","publisher":"pakistani.org (text of the Constitution of Pakistan)","publication_date":"n.d.","url":"https://www.pakistani.org/pakistan/constitution/part3.ch3.html","claim":"The federal executive authority is exercised through the Prime Minister, who is the chief executive of the Federation, and the Prime Minister is elected by the National Assembly.","locator":"Article 90(1) and Article 91(3)-(5)","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct constitutional text from the governing legal framework.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly applicable to 2022 office authority and parliamentary dependence.","ruler_attribution":"Defines Shehbaz Sharif's formal office and confidence dependence, but not personal control of nuclear policy.","contrary_evidence":["Formal chief-executive status does not prove direct operational control over nuclear forces or security institutions."],"lenses":["1B.7","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Chapter 2: Armed Forces","publisher":"pakistani.org (text of the Constitution of Pakistan)","publication_date":"n.d.","url":"https://www.pakistani.org/pakistan/constitution/part12.ch2.html","claim":"The Federal Government has control and command of the Armed Forces.","locator":"Article 243(1)","source_type":"primary/legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct constitutional text on state command authority.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable framework in force during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Shows the formal state command structure under Sharif's government, but not a target-period nuclear decision by Sharif personally.","contrary_evidence":["This general command clause does not by itself show day-to-day nuclear command or how much practical autonomy the military retained."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"10. World nuclear forces","publisher":"SIPRI","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.sipri.org/yearbook/2022/10","claim":"India and Pakistan also seem to be increasing the size of their nuclear weapon inventories, and transparency remains low for both countries.","locator":"World nuclear forces overview, nuclear arsenals and low levels of transparency sections","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"SIPRI is a leading open-source nuclear forces assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the 2022 inherited nuclear posture and risk environment.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual inherited posture only; it does not attribute the build-up to Sharif personally.","contrary_evidence":["Open-source estimates are approximate and do not prove a 2022 Sharif decision."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"n.d.","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/resources/information-materials/frequently-asked-questions","claim":"Pakistan has not yet signed the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty, so the treaty has not entered into force for Pakistan.","locator":"CTBT entry-into-force FAQ, paragraph on Annex 2 states","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CTBTO is the treaty organization and states the treaty status directly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable treaty posture in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Shows inherited treaty status under Sharif, not a Sharif-initiated change.","contrary_evidence":["Not signing the CTBT does not by itself prove intent to test or a deliberate risk increase in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Arms Control And Disarmament","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"n.d.","url":"https://www.mofa.gov.pk/arms-control-and-disarmament","claim":"Pakistan says it has consistently supported the CTBT, observes a unilateral moratorium on nuclear testing, and will not be the first to resume testing in South Asia.","locator":"CTBT section and FMCT section","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official policy page, but it is self-descriptive and not an independent verification of implementation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable official posture relevant to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Useful for Sharif-era inherited posture and public stance, but not proof of a 2022 executive decision by Sharif.","contrary_evidence":["Official posture is not the same as a legally binding treaty commitment or evidence of behavior beyond rhetoric."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.4"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Youm-e-Takbeer, a day of nation’s remarkable journey towards credible minimum deterrence: PM","publisher":"Associated Press of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-05-28","url":"https://www.app.com.pk/national/youm-e-takbeer-a-day-of-nations-remarkable-journey-towards-credible-minimum-deterrence-pm/","claim":"Shehbaz Sharif said Youm-e-Takbeer was Pakistan's journey toward credible minimum deterrence and that Pakistan had set a redline for its defense and the 'rules of the game' for peace and stability in the region.","locator":"lead paragraphs and quoted tweet excerpt","source_type":"official media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"State media carried the PM's own dated public remarks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period and attributable to Sharif.","ruler_attribution":"This is Sharif's public framing of nuclear posture; it is rhetoric, not implementation evidence.","contrary_evidence":["Celebratory deterrence rhetoric does not itself show a concrete risk-reducing policy change."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.2","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Transcript of the Press Briefing by Spokesperson on Friday, 18 March, 2022","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-03-18","url":"https://mofa.gov.pk/transcript-of-the-press-briefing-by-spokesperson-on-friday-18-march-2022","claim":"After India's accidental missile launch, Pakistan demanded a joint probe and raised serious concerns about security protocols and technical safeguards against accidental or unauthorized launch in a nuclearized environment.","locator":"Q&A and response paragraphs on the missile incident","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dated official transcript of the government's response in the crisis moment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period and directly about a 2022 crisis.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the government under Sharif, though the transcript is from the foreign ministry rather than Sharif personally.","contrary_evidence":["The response was also highly accusatory, so it is mixed evidence: de-escalatory in process but escalatory in tone."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.8","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India says reviewing procedures after missile accidentally fired into Pakistan","publisher":"Dawn / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-03-15","url":"https://www.dawn.com/news/1680101","claim":"India said it was reviewing procedures after accidentally firing a missile into Pakistan during routine maintenance.","locator":"lead paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting is a strong independent corroborator of the incident context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period, corroborating the March 2022 crisis context.","ruler_attribution":"Context only; the incident originated in India, not from Sharif's own action.","contrary_evidence":["This does not show Sharif's conduct directly, only the external crisis he had to manage."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN Disarmament Fellows’ visit to Pakistan 8-10 October 2022","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://mofa.gov.pk/un-disarmament-fellows-visit-to-pakistan-8-10-october-2022","claim":"Pakistan hosted UN Disarmament Fellows and briefed them on arms control, disarmament, nuclear non-proliferation, dual-use technologies, and Pakistan's nuclear safety and security architecture, regulatory mechanisms, and safeguards.","locator":"body paragraphs describing the ministry briefing and PCENS/PNRA visits","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dated official event summary with concrete institutional content.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in-period and relevant to 2022 institutional posture.","ruler_attribution":"Shows the state's in-period institutional messaging, not a unique personal decision by Sharif.","contrary_evidence":["Hosting a briefing is not the same as proving a new policy, institutional reform, or reduced risk."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan's Evolving Nuclear Doctrine","publisher":"Arms Control Association","publication_date":"2023-10-01","url":"https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2023-10/features/pakistans-evolving-nuclear-doctrine","claim":"Pakistan reportedly keeps its nuclear weapons de-mated, with a strict centralized command-and-control system in place, and maintains a unilateral moratorium on nuclear testing.","locator":"doctrine discussion on de-mated weapons, centralized command and control, and testing moratorium","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Strong secondary synthesis, but retrospective and not a 2022 primary record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Useful inherited-structure context for 2022, though written later.","ruler_attribution":"Background only; it describes a standing doctrine and security architecture rather than a Sharif-specific 2022 decision.","contrary_evidence":["This remains a long-standing doctrine summary, not evidence that Sharif improved or worsened it in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Arms Control and Proliferation Profile: Pakistan","publisher":"Arms Control Association","publication_date":"2023-08-01","url":"https://www.armscontrol.org/factsheets/arms-control-and-proliferation-profile-pakistan","claim":"Pakistan developed nuclear weapons outside the NPT and is believed to possess an arsenal of about 170 nuclear warheads as of 2022, while continuing to build up its force and develop new delivery systems.","locator":"opening overview on nuclear arsenal and force build-up","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Well-regarded secondary source summarizing open-source assessments, though still retrospective.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Strong inherited-posture context for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual inherited risk posture, not direct evidence of a Sharif decision.","contrary_evidence":["Open-source arsenal estimates are approximate and do not attribute the build-up to Sharif personally."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.5","1B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary
- I screened roughly 30 documents across constitutional text, CTBTO, IAEA, SIPRI, Arms Control Association, MOFA, APP, Reuters/Dawn/AP, and UN material.
- I kept only sources that either define Sharif's formal authority, establish Pakistan's inherited nuclear posture, or document a 2022 crisis response.
- I rejected duplicates and syndications: multiple Reuters copies of the March 2022 missile incident, multiple APP copies of the same Youm-e-Takbeer message, and later 2025-2026 APP items that were out of period for 2022.
- I rejected weakly relevant pages that did not add to 1B attribution or exposure, including general Pakistan pages, broad political items, and non-nuclear commentary.
- I tried to access direct official NCA Act text on Pakistan code / law mirrors, but the fetch failed, so I relied on constitutional text and higher-quality institutional summaries instead.
- I also set aside several later retrospective explainers that were useful for context but not clean target-period evidence, including post-2022 doctrinal explainers and later official rhetoric.

Remaining gaps
- I did not find a direct 2022 primary record of Sharif personally chairing a nuclear command meeting, issuing a nuclear-use directive, or altering doctrine, safeguards, or treaty posture.
- I did not find evidence of any 2022 Sharif decision on proliferation, cyber, biological, or other catastrophic dual-use risk domains.
- I found only one concrete 2022 crisis episode with a nuclearized context, the March missile incident, and the available evidence is mostly government response plus independent corroboration of the incident, not a deeper internal decision record.
- More search would only help materially if there are archived PMO, National Command Authority, or cabinet records that are not surfaced in open web results; otherwise the chapter is likely to remain a limited-exposure, largely inherited-posture dossier.
